# No trailing slash, deliberately. `node_modules/` matches a DIRECTORY and not a
# SYMLINK to one — and a scratch worktree that links back to the parent's install is
# exactly how a self-referencing `node_modules -> .` got committed, taking the real
# install with it. The review loop builds its submission with `git add -A`, so an
# unignored symlink here reaches a reviewed tree and then origin.
node_modules
dist/
*.tsbuildinfo

# per-branch finding ledger, written into a reviewed worktree (SPEC §4.3)
.rev/

# The LOCAL deployment's data directory: database, bare clones, worktrees.
#
# It lives inside the repo for convenience on a laptop, which means git would
# otherwise offer to commit the knowledge base and every reviewed worktree —
# including other people's repositories.
lore/

# Agent worktrees. Each is a full checkout with its own node_modules — 1.1G here
# during one workflow run — and git worktrees are NOT ignored automatically when
# they live inside the parent repo.
#
# This matters more than it looks: the review loop builds its submission with
# `git add -A && git write-tree`, so an unignored worktree directory would put
# every agent's checkout into the tree hash lore verifies against.
.claude/

.DS_Store
*.log
.env
.env.*
# `.env.*` ABOVE SWALLOWS ITS OWN TEMPLATE — found by lore's own review, fingerprint
# 2146b6dd: README.md's quick start says `cp .env.example .env`, but `.env.example`
# matches `.env.*` too, so `git add` (and `git add -A`, which the review loop itself
# uses to build a submission) silently drops it. The template existed on disk and
# had never once been committed — a new operator following the README got "No such
# file or directory" on the very first command, with no tracked file anywhere to fix
# it from. Un-ignored explicitly, by exact name rather than a wildcard, so nothing
# else named `*.env.*` (a real per-machine override) is re-included by accident.
!.env.example

# Staged opencode credentials and deployment secrets. Never committed.
deploy/opencode/
deploy/.env

# Project-local MCP wiring. Points at a private host and is per-machine, so it
# does not belong in a public repository even though the token itself is an env
# reference rather than a literal.
.mcp.json
